From 2d832d87644bbf2272d17c9f335840d73ed894dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 10:17:40 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 178/274] Sink detector and draft-zero rejection: verify each
 step's last sampled token as draft zero of the next

A generated token can turn into an attention sink (DeepSeek V4.1 Flash: the residual's
column 2455 runs away from layer 20 on, to 1.1e5-1.3e5 by the last layer), and the row's
next-token distribution becomes document-start noise: `How can\n can I help`. The forward
pass is the model's own, so this is a decoding policy, on model runner V2 (the runner the
DSpark recipe uses; the V1 runner is untouched).

With VLLM_SINK_REJECTION=1 the last token a step samples is not committed. The scheduler
holds it as the request's provisional token and schedules it as draft zero of the next
step, ahead of the drafter's tokens (SchedulerOutput.sink_draft_zero_req_ids); the V2
runner's inputs already are [last sampled token, drafts]. So it goes through the existing
machinery: the rejected-draft accounting of computed tokens and async placeholders, prefix
caching of committed tokens only, the grammar accepting committed tokens only, the frontend
receiving committed tokens only. A held token that is EOS, a stop token or the max-tokens or
max-model-len token is committed when sampled, unverified. Preemption drops it.

The verifier accepts draft zero unless its own row is sunk; a sunk draft zero is rejected
with all its step's rows and replaced by the next alternate of the draw that produced it
(the top of the same scores after the drawn token: logits, or logits plus the same Gumbel
noise, so sampling without replacement), with that alternate's logprob and rank in the
draw, at most VLLM_SINK_REJECTION_MAX_RETRIES times; past them it is committed and counted
as a fallthrough. A sunk drafter token cuts the verification: its logit is masked in the
row that verifies it before the kernels run, and restored before logprobs. The alternates
are kept per request slot on the device; on the all-greedy fast path each rank's top
candidates of the last rows are gathered (8 floats a request). A request whose draft zero
was rejected has no valid row: DSpark/DFlash anchor its replacement at draft zero's position.
The candidates of each draw and the policy are one Triton kernel each; the policy takes a
rejected draft zero out of the request state before the update appends its replacement.
The flags, the cut and the merge of the candidates are sync-free torch operations.
FlashInfer sampling stays on with rejection off.

The detector reads the watched column of each residual stream, which the SM120 V4.1 model
(vllm/models/deepseek_v4_1, the one GB10 loads) keeps as [tokens, 4] float32
(get_sink_residual_columns), gathered like its hidden states under sequence parallelism and
CED row compaction; the runner refuses models without it. Default threshold 85000, the
geometric midpoint between the largest ordinary row of a 358,785-token served survey
(62,720, number tokens) and the smallest sunk row (108,032 on DeepSeek's CPU reference;
116,736-118,784 served). Dead verification rows are not flagged.
VLLM_SINK_DETECTOR_CHECK_RANKS=1 checks the tensor-parallel ranks' flags every step (lab
only). The sampling-only settings stay out of the compile cache key.

Surfaced as vllm:sink_flagged_rows{row=draft_zero|drafter|prompt},
vllm:sink_draft_zero_rejections, vllm:sink_draft_cuts, vllm:sink_fallthroughs, the periodic
stats line, a scheduler log line per event (the largest flagged |column| value and the output
position of its row), per-rank counts in each worker's log, and
usage.sink_rejection on every response with the detector on. The V2 runner refuses
rejection with the features it does not handle (pipeline parallelism, batch-sharded
sampling, PCP, DBO, other drafters, probabilistic drafts, watermarking, sampling masks,
trace replay, routed experts, KV transfer, boundary checkpoints). The warmup's synthetic
decode steps carry draft zero; their rows (token 0, DeepSeek V4.1's BOS, the sink token)
are flagged but neither logged nor counted.

Tests: the scheduler and frontend with a scripted worker (sync and async: the collapse
sequence by draft-zero rejection and by drafter cut, fallthrough, a rejection in the middle
of a batch, zero drafter tokens, the accounting, EOS/stop/max-tokens tokens, abort while
held, logprobs, n > 1, stream intervals, the grammar, the async back-fill, preemption with
a stale output, encoding); on the GPU the detector on the reference numbers, the
alternates as the draw's next samples, the cut, the policy, the request-state update, the
drafter inputs after a rejection, and the collapse sequence through the real sampler and
rejection sampler (greedy and random, block verification) with its batch neighbours
unchanged. The tokenizer, parser, sampling, verification, drafter-input and scheduler
suites fail exactly where the untouched image fails offline.

